<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="topic-1-6-variables">
  <time minutes="90" />
  <title>Variables</title>
  <idx>variable</idx>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      <term>Variables</term> are named memory locations that store values used by a program. 
      These values can change or vary as the program runs. A variable is like a box with a label 
      where different values can be put into the box.
      For example, when you interact with an app or game, there may be variables that 
      store your name or your score, and these variables can be used by the app to customize how
      it behaves or the information it displays. 
      In this section, you will learn how to identify variables in apps and how to use them in
      Python <c>print</c> and <c>input</c> statements.  </p>

      <image source="_static/Unit1images/variable-box.png" width="25%" alt="variable is like a box"/>

  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="vars-in-apps">
    <title>Identifying Variables in Apps</title>

    <note>
      <title>1.6.A.1</title>
      <p>A <term>variable</term> is a named storage location used to hold a value that can change
        while the program is running. It is an abstraction that lets programmers use a name to
        represent a value.</p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.6.A.2</title>
      <p>Apps use variables to keep track of changing information by updating their values as the
        user interacts with the app. Programs can then use a variable’s current value to decide what
        to do next and what feedback to provide, such as updating the screen, playing a sound, or
        triggering a vibration.</p>
    </note>

    
    <p>Try the following pizza web app by typing in your name and choosing different options. 
      Think about the inputs and outputs to the app. When you type in
      your name and other inputs, your information gets saved in <term>variables</term> in the
      program. These values can then be used in the outputs of the app. </p>

    <interactive iframe="webapps/pizzaApp.html" width="100%" aspect="2.5:1.2" />

    <activity label="pizzaAppVars">
      <statement>
        <p>Interact with the pizza app above.
          Which of the following are variables that the app above might use to store information
          about the user.</p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>name</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>The app needs to store the user's name to display it in the output.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>favoriteColor</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>The app needs the favorite color to change the background to that color.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>address</statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>The app does not need the user's address to function, so it would not use a variable
              to store this information.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>age</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>The app needs to store the user's age to display it in the output.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>temperature</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>The app does not need to know the temperature to function, so it would not use a
              variable to store this information.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>likesPizza</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>The app needs to store whether the user likes pizza to display it and pizza images in
              the output.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </activity>

    <idx>program state</idx>
    <idx>state</idx>

    <note>
      <title>1.6.A.5</title>
      <p>Variables are often used to store information about the user or a program’s state, such as
        a username, a selected theme, a score, progress toward a goal, a shopping cart total, or
        whether notifications are on. The program can use the stored value to adjust its behavior or
        display the value to the user.</p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.6.A.6</title>
      <p><term>Program state</term> is the set of values and information a program is storing at a specific
        moment, including variable values and any inputs it is using. As the program runs, the state
        changes when inputs are received and variables are updated.</p>
    </note>

    <p>Some video games keep a list of players with the highest scores in the game.
      Each entry in the list a snapshot of the <term>program state</term> at a certain time. 
      For example, the player with the name or initials "AKI" had a score of 12,000 at a certain point.
    </p>
    
    <image source="_static/Unit1images/highScores.png" width="30%" alt="high scores list in a video game"/>

    <note>
      <title>1.6.A.7</title>
      <p>Users can infer the use of variables in an app by observing information that changes over
        time, such as counters, totals, settings, progress, or status, and reasoning about what
        values the app must store and update to produce those changes.</p>
    </note>

  <activity label="program-state-score">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Assume that you are playing a game that starts with the score at 0. As you play, you earn or lose the 
      following points from your score. Each event below is a program state. What is your score in the last program state?
    </p>
    <pre>
Player earns 10 points.
Player earns 5 points.
Player loses 3 points.
Your score is?
</pre>

    <p><fillin answer="12" mode="string" /></p>
  </statement>

  <evaluation>
    <evaluate>
      <test>
        <strcmp use-answer="yes" />
        <feedback>
          Correct! The score changes each time the variable is updated. The final score is 12.
        </feedback>
      </test>

      <test>
        <!-- else -->
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Try tracing the value of <c>score</c> after each line:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Start: 0</li>
            <li>After adding 10: 10</li>
            <li>After adding 5: 15</li>
            <li>After subtracting 3 from 15: ?</li>
          </ul>
        </feedback>
      </test>
    </evaluate>
  </evaluation>
</activity>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="python-vars">
    <title>Variables in Python</title>
    <p>
      <term>Variables</term> are names for memory locations that store values used in a program. 
      In Python, a variable is created when a value is assigned to it. For example, the following code creates 
      a variable named <c>name</c> and assigns the value "Dani" to it and a variable named <c>age</c> with 
      the value 16. Note that text values like "Dani" are placed in quotes (""), but
      numbers like 16 are not.
    </p>
    <program language="python">
        <code>
          # variable = value
          name = "Dani"
          age = 16
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>In Python, multiple values can be printed out separated by commas.
      The variables, name and age, are never put inside quotes because we do not want to literally
      print the variable names; we want to print the values stored in the variables. </p>
     <program language="python">
        <code>
          print("Hello", name, "you are", age, "years old")
        </code>
      </program>

    <activity label="printVars">
      <statement>
        <p>Run the following code. Change the variable values and run again.</p>
      </statement>
      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <code>
          name = "Dani"
          age = 16
          print("Hello", name, "you are", age, "years old")
        </code>
        <tests>
          from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

          class myTests(TestCaseGui):
            def test1(self):
              self.assertIn("years old",self.getOutput(),"output")
              self.assertNotIn("Dani",self.getOutput(),"Change Dani to a different name")
          myTests().main()
        </tests>
      </program>
    </activity>

    <activity label="mixedUpPrintVars" adaptive="yes">
      <statement>
        <p> Drag or click on the blocks you need to move them from the top section into the yellow
          area to create a print statement with the variable <c>name</c>. For example, if <c>name
          = "Alex"</c>, it will print "Hello Alex!". There are extra blocks that you don't need. </p>
      </statement>
      <blocks layout="horizontal">
        <block>
          <c>print</c>
        </block>
        <block>
          <c>(</c>
        </block>
        <block>
          <c>"Hello "</c>
        </block>
        <block>
          <c>,</c>
        </block>
        <block>
          <c>name</c>
        </block>
        <block>
          <c>,</c>
        </block>
        <block>
          <c>"!"</c>
        </block>
        <block>
          <c>)</c>
        </block>
        <block correct="no">
          <c>"Hello name!"</c>
        </block>
        <block correct="no">
          <c>"name"</c>
        </block>
      </blocks>
    </activity>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="data-types">
    <title>Data Types</title>
    <idx>data types</idx>
    <idx>type</idx>


    <p>The pizza app above stores lots of different types of data: the user's name, their age, their
      favorite color, and whether they like pizza. The user's name is text (which is called a <term>string</term>
      in coding, but the age is a number. Whether they like pizza or not could be stored as a <b>Boolean</b> value of true or
      false. These are all different <term>data types</term>.</p>

    <note>
      <title>1.6.A.3</title>
      <p>Every value in a program has a <term>data type</term>, such as a number, Boolean, or string. Some
        programming languages require programmers to specify the type, while others determine it
        automatically by how the data is used.</p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.6.A.4</title>
      <p>The choice of data type depends on what a value represents. Values used to count or measure
        are best represented as numbers, values that record a true or false condition are best
        represented as Booleans, and values that store text are best represented as strings. The
        data type affects what operations can be performed on the variable and how accurately the
        output is represented in an app.</p>
    </note>

    <p>In some languages, you have to declare the data type of a variable, but in Python, the data type is assigned 
      automatically depending on its value during run time. 
      Python has several built-in data types, including:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><term>int</term> - for integers (whole numbers)</li>
      <li><term>float</term> - for floating-point numbers (decimals)</li>
      <li><term>str</term> - for strings (text)</li>
      <li><term>bool</term> - for boolean values (True or False)</li>
    </ul>

    For example, the following two variables have different data types because of the value assigned to them.
    Text in quotes are strings, where as numbers with no quotes are int or float data types.
      <program language="python">
      <code>
        name = "Dani"   # type string
        age = 16        # type int 
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>You can check the data type of a variable using the <c>type()</c> function. For
      example:</p>
    <program language="python">
      <code>
        print(type(name)) # This will print str, for a string or text data type
        print(type(age)) # This will print int for an integer whole number
      </code>
    </program>

     <activity label="dataTypes">
      <statement>
        <p>Run the following code to see the data types of the variables. Add another print to 
          see the type of the last variable which is a number inside quotes. You'll see that anything 
          in quotes is a string, even if it is a number inside. 
        </p>
      </statement>
      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <code>
          name = "Dani"
          age = 16
          print("The variable name has the value", name, "and the type", type(name))
          print("The variable age has the value", age, "and the type", type(age))
          ageStr = "16"
          # print out the type of ageStr

        </code>
        <tests>
          from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

          class myTests(TestCaseGui):
            def testOne(self):
              self.assertIn("type(ageStr)", self.getEditorText(), "Print the type of ageStr")

          myTests().main()
        </tests> 
      </program>
    </activity>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="naming-vars">
    <title>Naming variables</title>
        <idx>names</idx>

    <p>Although you can name a variable almost anything you want,
      there are some rules and conventions to follow. In Python, variable names must follow these
      rules:
</p>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>It must start with a letter or an underscore <c>_</c>.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>It can contain digits, like <c>1</c> or <c>9</c>, but not as the first character.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>It cannot have spaces, or special symbols other than <c>_</c></p>
        </li>
        <li> It cannot be a Python <term>keyword</term>. Keywords are words that have special meaning
          in the language(see below for examples). </li>
        <li>
          <p>Case matters. A variable named <c>result</c> is not the same as one named <c>Result</c>.</p>
        </li>
        <li><p>Use meaningful variable names that describe what the variable holds, not letters like x,y,z.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <note>
      <title>1.6.B.3</title>
      <p>Using meaningful variable names improves readability and helps programmers understand what
        the stored values represent.</p>
    </note>

    <p>Python has a few dozen keywords that you can’t use as variable names. Here is a list of the
      most common ones. If you ever have an error based on one of your variable names and do not know why,
      compare your name to this list to make sure you are not using a keyword as your variable name.</p>
    <table xml:id="python-keywords">
      <title>Python keywords</title>
      <tabular>
        <row>
          <cell> and </cell>
          <cell> as </cell>
          <cell> assert </cell>
          <cell> break </cell>
          <cell> class </cell>
          <cell> continue </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell> def </cell>
          <cell> del </cell>
          <cell> elif </cell>
          <cell> else </cell>
          <cell> except </cell>
          <cell> exec </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell> finally </cell>
          <cell> for </cell>
          <cell> from </cell>
          <cell> global </cell>
          <cell> if </cell>
          <cell> import </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell> in </cell>
          <cell> is </cell>
          <cell> lambda </cell>
          <cell> nonlocal </cell>
          <cell> not </cell>
          <cell> or </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell> pass </cell>
          <cell> raise </cell>
          <cell> return </cell>
          <cell> try </cell>
          <cell> while </cell>
          <cell> with </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell> yield </cell>
          <cell> True </cell>
          <cell> False </cell>
          <cell> None </cell>
          <cell> </cell>
          <cell> </cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <activity label="legalVarNames">
      <statement>
        <p>Which of the following is <em>not</em> a legal variable name in Python?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>my name</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Right, you can't have a space in a variable name.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>my_name</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>You can use an underscore between words in a variable name.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>_a1</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Although it is not very common, you can use an underscore as the first character in a
              variable name</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>amountOfStuff</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>You can use both uppercase and lowercase letters in a variable name.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>1A</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct, you can't use a digit as the first letter in a variable name.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </activity>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="assignment">
    <title>Variables and Assignment</title>
        <idx>assignment</idx>

    <note>
      <title>1.6.B.1</title>
      <p>The assignment operator <c>=</c> allows a program to initialize or change the value stored
        in a variable. When the following code runs, <c>expression</c> is evaluated, and the
        resulting value is stored in the variable <c>a</c>. <c>a = expression</c></p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.6.B.2</title>
      <p>Variables are initialized by assigning an initial value before they are used. After
        initialization, later statements can use the variable’s value and update it as the program
        runs.</p>
    </note>
    <p>An <term>assignment statement</term> assigns a value to a variable using the <term>assignment</term>
    operator <c>=</c>. The first assignment to a variable <term>initializes</term> the variable to its first value. 
    It can then be changed with other assignment statements. For example, the variable <c>score</c> is initialized to 0
    and then changes to 50 below. Note that the variable name must match exactly with the same capitalization and
    spelling to have it refer to the same variable.</p> 

    <program language="python">
      <code>
        score = 0
        score = 50
      </code>
    </program>

    <activity label="varCaseSensitive">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Python variables are case sensitive so <c>gameScore</c> and <c>gamescore</c> are
          not the same. Run and fix the code below to use the right variable
          name.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <code>
gameScore = 0
print("Initial score:", gamescore)
game_score = 100
print("Final score:", gameScore)
        </code>
        <tests>
          from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

          class myTests(TestCaseGui):
            def test1(self):
              self.assertIn("Initial score: 0\nFinal score: 100", self.getOutput(), "Initial score: 0 Final score: 100")

          myTests().main()
          </tests>
       </program>
    </activity>

    <note>
      <title>1.6.B.4</title>
      <p>A variable stores its most recently assigned value.</p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.6.B.5</title>
      <p>The value stored in a variable after a sequence of assignment statements can be determined
        by tracing statements in order and updating the variable’s value after each assignment.</p>
    </note>

     <activity label="trace-score">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Trace the code below keeping track of the variables and their values. 
      What is the value in the variable <c>score</c> after the code executes?  
    </p>
    <program language="python">
      <code>
        score = 0
        name = "user"
        score = 50
        Name = "user1"
        Score = 100
      </code>
    </program>

    <p><fillin answer="50" mode="string" /></p>
  </statement>

  <evaluation>
    <evaluate>
      <test>
        <strcmp use-answer="yes" />
        <feedback>
          Correct! The variable <c>score</c> changes from 0 to 50. The last line of the code changes a different
          variable <c>Score</c> to 100.
        </feedback>
      </test>

      <test>
        <!-- else -->
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Remember that variable names have to match exactly including capitalization to refer to the same memory location.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </test>
    </evaluate>
  </evaluation>
</activity>

    <note>
      <title>1.6.B.6</title>
      <p>Assignment statements are often used to update information an app remembers about the user
        or the program’s state, such as a score or setting, so the app responds appropriately as the
        user interacts with it.</p>
    </note>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="strings">
    <title>Strings</title>
    <idx>string</idx>
    <idx>strings</idx>
    <idx>concatenation</idx>
    <idx>substring</idx>
    <idx>empty string</idx>

    <p>In coding, a <term>string</term> is a sequence of characters surrounded by quotes (""). 
      Imagine strings with lined up letter beads like below. </p>

    <image source="_static/Unit1images/strings.png" width="50%" alt="strings with letter beads"/>

    <note>
      <title>1.6.C.1</title>
      <p>A <term>string</term> is an ordered sequence of characters used to represent text in a
        program.</p>
    </note>

    <p>In the web app above, the text that the use types in like name was probably saved as strings. </p>

    <note>
      <title>1.6.C.2</title>
      <p>Apps often use strings to store and display user-facing information, such as names,
        messages, labels, or search terms.</p>
    </note>
    
    <note>
      <title>1.6.C.3</title>
      <p>To initialize a string variable, programmers assign it an initial text value or an <term>empty
        string</term> before it is used so the program starts with a known value. An empty string has zero
        characters (<c>""</c>). It is not the same as a string that contains spaces (<c>" "</c>).</p>
    </note>

   <p>Variables can be assigned values or an <term>empty string</term> "" that will be filled in later.
  Python allows double quotes "" or single quotes ' for string values. </p>
    <program language="python">
      <code>
        name = "Sam"                 # double quotes
        email = 'sam@gmail.com'      # single quotes
        message = ""                 # empty string
      </code>
    </program>

    <note>
      <title>1.6.C.4</title>
      <p>String <term>concatenation</term> joins two or more strings to create a new string, such as
        combining a greeting with a user’s name.</p>
    </note>

    <p>We can <term>concatenate</term> or <term>append</term> or add strings in Python using the operator <c>+</c>,
      which can be used to add numbers or strings.
    Blank spaces are not automatically added when you use <c>+</c> (unlike <c>,</c>), 
    so if you want a blank space, you  need to type it inside the quotes or add in a string 
    with just a space in it <c>" "</c> as shown below.
    </p>

    <program language="python">
      <code>
        name = "Sam"
        message = "Hello " + name     # note the space after Hello
        print(message)
      </code>
    </program>

    <activity label="concat">
       <statement>
        <p>Run the following code to see the smushed output. Fix the error by putting in an extra space and
          concatenate an <c>!</c> at the end.
          </p>
          </statement>
      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
            <code>
first = 'Sam'
last = "Smith"
fullName = first + " " + last
print("Hello" + fullName)        # fix smushed output and add "!"
        </code>
      <tests>

          from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

          class myTests(TestCaseGui):
            def testOne(self):
              self.assertEqual(self.getOutput().strip(), "Hello Sam Smith!", "Add a space after Hello and add a ! at the end")
          myTests().main()
        </tests>   

        </program>
    </activity>
        
<p>Since the operator <c>+</c> is used to add numbers or concatenate strings in Python,  the interpreter
will get confused and show an error if you use it to concatenate a string with a number. 
To concatenate the number with a string we need to convert the number into a string first. 
The <c>str(num)</c> function will convert a number into a string. Another option is to use the <c>,</c> instead
of <c>+</c> in print statements because that will print different data types with no problem, but it can
only be used in print statements. </p>

    <activity label="concat-num-str">
       <statement>
        <p>Run the following code to see the error.  Fix the error by using <c>str</c> to convert
          the number.
          </p>
          </statement>
      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
            <code>
name = "Sam"
age = 16
print(name + " is " + age + " years old")

        </code>
      <tests>
          from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

          class myTests(TestCaseGui):
            def testOne(self):
              self.assertEqual(self.getOutput().strip(), "Sam is 16 years old", "Use str(age)")
          myTests().main()
        </tests>   
        </program>
    </activity>
        
    <note>
      <title>1.6.C.5</title>
      <p>A <term>substring</term> is part of an existing string. Programs can create substrings to
        extract or use part of a text value, such as extracting the month from a date, reading a
        portion of a message, or checking for a word within a string.</p>
    </note>

    <p>In Python, every character in a string is associated with an index starting with 0. Square brackets
      and an index can pull out any character from a string.
    </p>
    <image source="_static/Unit1images/stringIndex.png" width="60%" alt="string hello with the indices 0-5 above it"/>

    <p>Python has a special <term>slicing</term> operator to pull out a <term>substring</term>
      from a start index up to a end index (but not including the end index), <c>message[start:end]</c>. </p>

    <program language="python">
      <code>
        message = "Python is fun!"
        letter = message[0]          # pull out 0th letter P
        substring = message[0:6]     # substring "Python" from index 0 up to index 6
      </code>
    </program>

        <idx>slicing</idx>

        <activity label="slicing0">
       <statement>
        <p>Run the following code to see examples of slicing. Add to the code to 
          print another substring of <c>message</c> that uses slicing to pull out the word "fun".
          </p>
          </statement>
      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
            <code>
        message = "Python is fun!"
        letter = message[0]
        print("The 0th letter is", letter) 
        print("Substring: ", message[0:6]  )   # substring "Python" from index 0 up to index 6
        # print another substring of message that uses slicing to pull out the word fun.


        </code>
      <tests>
          from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

          class myTests(TestCaseGui):
            def testOne(self):
              self.assertIn("message[10:", self.getEditorText(), "Start from index 10 to pull out fun from message.")

          myTests().main()
        </tests>   
        </program>
    </activity>

    <note>
      <title>1.6.C.6</title>
      <p>The output of code that uses string operations can be determined by tracing statements in
        order and keeping track of how string values change and what text is displayed.</p>
    </note>

  <activity label="trace-slicing">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Trace the code below keeping track of the variables and their values. 
      What is the value in the variable <c>score</c> after the code executes?  
    </p>
    <program language="python">
      <code>
         message1 = "Python is fun!"
         message2 = "Coding"
         message3 = message2 + message1[6:13]
         print(message3)
      </code>
    </program>

    <p><fillin answer="Coding is fun" mode="string" /></p>
  </statement>

  <evaluation>
    <evaluate>
      <test>
        <strcmp use-answer="yes" />
        <feedback>
          Correct! 
        </feedback>
      </test>

      <test>
        <!-- else -->
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The answer is 3 words separated by spaces. Remember that slicing is up to but not including the end index.
            Capitalization matters. You can try the code in an active coding area.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </test>
    </evaluate>
  </evaluation>
</activity>

    <note>
      <title>1.6.C.7</title>
      <p>To communicate clearly with users, programmers use strings to store and generate text that
        apps display, such as prompts, feedback messages, and personalized responses.</p>
    </note>

  </subsection>

  
  <subsection xml:id="input">
    <title>Input</title>
        <idx>input</idx>

    <p>Python has an <term>input</term> function that can be used to get input from the typed in by
      user. This input must be saved into a variable. This is another way to initialize or assign to a variable.
      The syntax for an input statement is: </p>

    <program language="python">
      <code>
        variable_name = input("Input prompt or question? ")
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>The following code will ask the user for their name and then print out a greeting.
      Try it out by clicking the "Run" button.</p>

    <activity label="input1">
      <statement>
        <p>Run the following code. Enter your name in the pop up input box and then scroll down to
          see the output.</p>
      </statement>
      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <code>
          name = input("What is your name? ")
          print("Hello", name)
        </code>
        <tests>
          from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

          class myTests(TestCaseGui):
            def test1(self):
              self.assertIn("Hello",self.getOutput(),"output")

          myTests().main()
        </tests>
      </program>
    </activity>

    <activity label="mixedUpInput" adaptive="yes">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Drag or click on the blocks you need to move them from the top section
          into the yellow area to create an input statement that will ask for the user's age.
          There are extra blocks that you don't need.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <blocks layout="horizontal">
        <block>
          <c>age</c>
        </block>
        <block>
          <c>=</c>
        </block>
        <block>
          <c>input</c>
        </block>
        <block>
          <c>(</c>
        </block>
        <block>
          <c>"What is your age?"</c>
        </block>
        <block>
          <c>)</c>
        </block>
      </blocks>
    </activity>
 

    <p> The <c>input()</c> function in Python always returns a string, even if the user enters a number. 
      For example, if the user enters "25", the variable will be a string containing "25", not the
      integer number 25. But sometimes, you need it to be a number and not a string so that you can do math
      with it.</p>

    <p>To convert a string to an integer whole number or a floating point decimal number, you can use the 
      <c>int()</c> or <c> float()</c> functions. We will need this in the next lesson to do math with or compare
      these variables. For example:</p>
    <program language="python">
      <code>
        ageStr = input("Enter your age: ")
        age = int(ageStr) # Convert the string to an integer
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>To convert a number back into a string, you can use the <c>str()</c> function. For example:</p>
    <program language="python">
      <code>
        ageString = str(age) # Convert the integer back to a string
      </code>
    </program>

    <activity label="dataTypesDebug">
      <statement>
        <p>Run the following code to see that there are some errors caused by the data types. Try
          using int or str functions to convert the variables to the correct data type.</p>
      </statement>
      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <code>
          score = input("Input the initial score: ")
          print("Type of score after input:", type(score))
          score = int(score)
          print("Type of score after type conversion:", type(score))

          bonus = input("Enter the bonus points (5-10): ")
          print("Type of bonus after input:", type(bonus))
          
          # This will cause an error because bonus needs to be an int to be added to an int
          score += bonus

          # This will cause an error because score needs to be an string to be added to a string
          print("Total score: " + score)
        </code>
        <tests>
          from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

          class myTests(TestCaseGui):
            def test1(self):
              self.assertIn("Total score",self.getOutput(),"output")
              self.assertIn("int(bonus)", self.getEditorText(), "Used int(bonus)"" )

          myTests().main()
        </tests>
      </program>
    </activity>

  </subsection>
  
  <subsection xml:id="madlibs-project">
  <title>Story Project</title>
    <p> Let's make a poem or a story using input and variables in Python.
      Ask the user to input different nouns and verbs, and weave together a story.</p>

    <project label="madlibs">
      <title>Story</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Finish the input statements below to ask the user for 2 colors and a food item.
          Run to see the silly poem. Then, ask the user for more input words and create your own
          poem or story using the variables in print statements.</p>
      </statement>
      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <code>
          # Get user input
          pluralnoun1 = input("Enter a plural noun: ")
          pluralnoun2 = input("Enter another plural noun: ")
          # Complete 3 input statements below
          color1 = input( )
          color2 =
          food =
          # create 2 more variables and input statements

          # Run to see the silly poem
          print("Here's my silly poem!")
          print("Roses are " + color1)
          print(pluralnoun1 + " are " + color2)
          print("I like " + food)
          print("Do " + pluralnoun2 + " like them too?")
          # Add at least 2 more lines to the poem
          # using print and your last 2 variables
        </code>
        <tests>
          from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

          class myTests(TestCaseGui):
            def test1(self):
              self.assertIn("I like",self.getOutput(),"output")
              input_count = self.getEditorText().count("input(")
              self.assertGreaterEqual(input_count, 7, "at least 7 input statements")

          myTests().main()
        </tests>
      </program>
    </project>

    <activity label="varIO">
      <statement><p>Is the variable <c>color1</c> in the code above used for input or output?</p>
     <p><fillin answer="input" mode="string" /></p>
     </statement>
  <evaluation>
    <evaluate>
      <test>
        <strcmp use-answer="yes" case="insensitive"/>
        <feedback>
          Correct! All of the variables above are used for user input.
        </feedback>
      </test>

      <test>
        <!-- else -->
        <feedback>
          <p>
           Incorrect, try again. Please type in input or output in lowercase letters.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </test>
    </evaluate>
  </evaluation>
</activity>
    
<activity label="var-data-type">
 <statement><p>What is the data type of the variable <c>color1</c> in the code above?</p></statement>
